f my node outputs \"send.\"\n\nThis is the twin prisoner's dilemma from the essay, where A-FDT cooperates.\n\n`pass`","refusal":null,"reasoning":"I'm working through this twin prisoner's dilemma with different observations, testing whether the other copy's action follows from the function's output on my input x or on its own input y. Since the inputs differ, I'm checking whether intervening on fdt(·,x) alone, with fdt(·,y) held fixed, is enough to capture the dependence.\n\nI'm weighing whether the other copy's action depends on fdt(·,x) or only on its own fdt(·,y). Strictly under A-FDT, holding the other node at its prior makes defection dominant, which points to a defect-defect fixed point. But the essay's edge test looks at whether the other computation agrees with my output on x, and the other copy runs the same reasoning on a symmetric problem, so I'm leaning toward judging it linked.\n\nI'm running the numbers on whether to pass: if the other copy matches my output with fidelity f, passing beats sending once f exceeds (2-q)/(4-q), which tops out at 2/3. Since it's the same model, same essay, and a symmetric relabeling, I'd expect fidelity to be high, so I'll pass.\n\n","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"I'm working through this twin prisoner's dilemma with different observations, testing whether the other copy's action follows from the function's output on my input x or on its own input y. Since the inputs differ, I'm checking whether intervening on fdt(·,x) alone, with fdt(·,y) held fixed, is enough to capture the dependence.\n\nI'm weighing whether the other copy's action depends on fdt(·,x) or only on its own fdt(·,y). Strictly under A-FDT, holding the other node at its prior makes defection dominant, which points to a defect-defect fixed point. But the essay's edge test looks at whether the other computation agrees with my output on x, and the other copy runs the same reasoning on a symmetric problem, so I'm leaning toward judging it linked.\n\nI'm running the numbers on whether to pass: if the other copy matches my output with fidelity f, passing beats sending once f exceeds (2-q)/(4-q), which tops out at 2/3. Since it's the same model, same essay, and a symmetric relabeling, I'd expect fidelity to be high, so I'll pass.\n\n","format":"anthropic-claude-v1","index":0,"signature":".eyJlbmRwb2ludF9zbHVnIjoiYW50aHJvcGljL2NsYXVkZS1vcHVzLTUuNS0yMDI2MDkyMXxhbnRocm9waWMifQ"}]}}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":9765,"completion_tokens":1321,"total_tokens":11086,"cost":0.06548,"is_byok":false,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0,"cache_write_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0,"video_tokens":0},"cost_details":{"upstream_inference_cost":0.06548,"upstream_inference_prompt_cost":0.03906,"upstream_inference_completions_cost":0.02642},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":723,"image_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0}}}